Here's Lauren, light it the mop.

162
00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:06.450
So a couple years ago, I started to notice that people were sounding quieter to me, which

163
00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:11.170
did not track with world events, but only when I was on the phone, and only when I had

164
00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:12.670
the phone to the right side of my head.

165
00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:14.210
And it wasn't, like, just that.

166
00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.250
I also noticed, like, I would get dizzy sometimes, and sometimes I kind of felt like my head was

167
00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:18.670
underwater.

168
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:24.530
So I held off, but eventually I made an appointment with, like, an ear, nose, and throat specialist

169
00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.970
and got my hearing tested.

170
00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:31.970
And I found out exactly what I did not want to know, which was that at, like, 32, I was losing

171
00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:32.430
my hearing.

172
00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:33.730
But only on one side.

173
00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:39.550
And he sort of, my doctor started going through a list of, like, other tests I could try and

174
00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:44.250
possible diagnoses, and I interrupted him and said, did I tell you my dad has Meniere's

175
00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:44.530
disease?

176
00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.490
And he looked at me, you know, like, how you do when you're looking at the answer to a

177
00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:49.070
trivia question.

178
00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:53.030
My dad lost his hearing completely within, like, 10 years of being diagnosed.

179
00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:57.550
So I knew that if I had the same deal, like, the same thing could happen to me.

180
00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.430
You know, I'd probably lose the rest of my hearing on my right side and maybe both

181
00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:07.790
years, like, within a few years or sooner or later or not at all.

182
00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:13.090
And usually I'm not, like, the kind of person who's like, oh, I gotta know what's gonna happen

183
00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:16.050
because I'm not picking the restaurant, okay?

184
00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:18.130
I'm not, like, a type A person.

185
00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:21.230
I do not have what the teens call main character energy.

186
00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:26.670
But in this moment, like, I wish that I did because there's not really a treatment for

187
00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:27.110
Meniere's.

188
00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:32.330
Like, you can take symptom stuff, so I could do, like, short-term steroids to ward off

189
00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:32.930
the vertigo.

190
00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:38.170
And my doctor told me to avoid caffeine and salt, which are, like, my two favorite food

191
00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:38.550
groups.

192
00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.910
So that weekend, I visited my parents.

193
00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.310
And it's never, like, nice to start a conversation with, what if I end up like you?

194
00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.810
But that's basically what I did to my dad.

195
00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.790
And he told me, you know, a few things.

196
00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.050
He was like, there's all this assistive technology and, like, supportive community for people

197
00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:56.850
with hearing loss.

198
00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:02.930
And frankly, it's not a huge tragedy not hear everybody all the time in this economy.

199
00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.750
My dad's a very, like, zen, very, like, believes in a higher path kind of person.

200
00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:12.090
So that kind of brought me out of my, like, basement of depression.

201
00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:16.190
But a few months later, I was back at the same doctor's office because I kept getting

202
00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.350
these episodes of, like, room-spinning vertigo.

203
00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:19.950
And my hearing was worse.

204
00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:21.190
So they retested me.

205
00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:26.270
And I was up to 80 decibels of loss in my right ear, which they have the little, like, dumb

206
00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.310
layman's term chart in the soundproof booth.

207
00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:34.610
So that means that on my right side, I can hear things that are louder than, quote, a lawnmower

208
00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:36.630
at close range, which is awesome.

209
00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.320
And so I went home.

210
00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.880
And I got on Reddit like you do when you're looking up rare diseases.

211
00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.560
And I found this interview with an artist who also had Meniere's disease.

